  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/congoglanis_alula.htm",
      "html": "<ul><li>Synonym: Doumea alula</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/congoglanis_alula.htm",
      "html": "<p>Africa: Western and central Africa: Congo River basin, Democratic Republic of the Congo and Central African Republic, and Angola. Type locality: Stanleyville, Congo.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/congoglanis_alula.htm",
      "html": "<p>Typically found in fastest flowing portions of rivers and always associated with large rocks; appears to congregate underneath rocks near where water breaks over the rocks during the day, and will move out onto the tops of rocks at night.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/congoglanis_alula.htm",
      "html": "<p>These catfish do not possess a swim bladder so they keep away from open waters.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/congoglanis_alula.htm",
      "html": "<p>Congoglanis alula differs from C. inga in having a shorter maxillary barbel not reaching to vertical through anterior margin of orbit, a longer pelvic fin (24–28% of standard length vs. 19–21%), a shorter postorbital length, a relatively narrow interorbital width and shorter anal-fin rays. It differs from C. sagitta in having a longer pectoral fin, a longer pelvic fin, a shorter and deeper caudal peduncle, a narrower interorbital width, a more posteriorly placed anal fin and fewer vertebrae (36–39 vs. 39–41). Dorsal soft rays: 8; anal soft rays: 9–11.</p><p>The specific name alula means winglet, perhaps referring to flattened and expanded wing-like fins, an adaptation to fast water.</p><p>IUCN Red List: Least Concern.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "references",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/amphiliidae/congoglanis_alula.htm",
      "html": "<p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., 2007. Checklist of catfishes, recent and fossil (Osteichthyes: Siluriformes), and catalogue of siluriform primary types. Zootaxa 1418:1-628.</p><p>Ferraris, C.J. Jr., R.P. Vari and P.H. Skelton, 2011. A new genus of African loach catfish (Siluriformes: Amphiliidae) from the Congo River basin, the sister-group to all other genera of the Doumeinae, with the description of two new species. Copeia 2011(4):477-489.</p><p>Froese, R. and D. Pauly. Editors. 2019. FishBase. www.fishbase.org (04/2019).</p><p>Moelants, T. 2010. Congoglanis alula. The IUCN Red List of Threatened Species 2010.</p><p>Seegers, L. 2008 The catfishes of Africa. A handbook for identification and maintenance. Aqualog Verlag A.C.S. GmbH, Germany. 604 p.</p><p>The ETYFish Project Fish Name Etymology Database © Christopher Scharpf.</p>"
    }
